You can solve this problem using the Java stream API by first filtering the list to only include numbers that appear more than once, and then finding the number that appears most often in the filtered list. Here's an example code snippet:
```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class MostFrequentNumber {
    public static int getMostFrequent(List<Integer> numbers) {
        // Filter the list to only include numbers that appear more than once
        List<Integer> filteredNumbers = numbers.stream()
                .filter(n -> Collections.frequency(numbers, n) > 1)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // Find the number that appears most often in the filtered list
        return filteredNumbers.stream()
                .max(Comparator.comparingInt(Collections::frequency))
                .get();
    }
}
```
In this code, we first filter the input list `numbers` to only include numbers that appear more than once using the `filter()` method. We then collect the remaining numbers into a new list using the `collect()` method and pass in a `Collectors.toList()` instance as the collector.

Next, we find the number that appears most often in the filtered list by first finding its frequency using the `frequency()` method of the `Collections` class. We then use the `max()` method to find the maximum element in the list based on this frequency and return it.

This solution uses only the stream API methods for basic operations like filtering, mapping, sorting, etc., without any additional data structures or libraries.

