Here is an example of a simple text editor written in JavaScript:
```
const fs = require('fs');

class TextEditor {
  constructor() {
    this.filePath = null;
    this.content = '';
  }

  open(filePath) {
    this.filePath = filePath;
    const data = fs.readFileSync(this.filePath, 'utf8');
    this.content = data.toString();
  }

  save() {
    if (this.filePath === null) {
      throw new Error('Cannot save without a file path');
    }

    fs.writeFileSync(this.filePath, this.content);
  }

  getContent() {
    return this.content;
  }

  setContent(newContent) {
    this.content = newContent;
  }
}
```
This class has the following methods:

* `open`: Opens a file and reads its content into memory. It takes a single argument, which is the path of the file to be opened.
* `save`: Saves the current content of the text editor to the file that was previously opened with `open`. If no file has been opened yet, it will throw an error.
* `getContent`: Returns the current content of the text editor.
* `setContent`: Sets the new content of the text editor. It takes a single argument, which is the new content to be set.

Here's an example usage of this class:
```
const editor = new TextEditor();
editor.open('example.txt');
console.log(editor.getContent()); // Output: "Hello World"
editor.setContent("This is a new content");
editor.save();
```
Note that this is just an example and there are many ways to improve the functionality of a text editor, such as adding support for multiple files, undo/redo functionality, etc.

