In this scenario, you are tasked with enhancing the functionality of a hash table by handling collisions. A collision occurs when two or more keys hash to the same index in the table. To handle collisions, you can use several techniques such as separate chaining, open addressing, and linear probing.

Here's an example of how you can implement separate chaining for a hash table:
```
class HashTable {
  constructor() {
    this.table = {};
  }

  put(key, value) {
    const index = this.hashCode(key);
    if (this.table[index] === undefined) {
      this.table[index] = [];
    }
    this.table[index].push({ key, value });
  }

  get(key) {
    const index = this.hashCode(key);
    return this.table[index];
  }

  hashCode(key) {
    // A simple hash function that returns a random number between 0 and the length of the table
    return Math.floor(Math.random() * this.table.length);
  }
}
```
In this example, we define a `HashTable` class with three methods: `put`, `get`, and `hashCode`. The `put` method takes two arguments, `key` and `value`, and stores them in the hash table using the `hashCode` function to determine the index where the key-value pair should be stored. If there is a collision, we store the new key-value pair in a separate chain by pushing it onto an array at the corresponding index in the table. The `get` method returns the value associated with the given key, or `undefined` if the key does not exist in the table.

To handle collisions using separate chaining, we can modify the `put` method as follows:
```
class HashTable {
  constructor() {
    this.table = {};
  }

  put(key, value) {
    const index = this.hashCode(key);
    if (this.table[index] === undefined) {
      this.table[index] = [];
    } else {
      // Check for collisions and separate chaining
      let currentIndex = index;
      while (this.table[currentIndex] !== undefined) {
        const collisionKey = this.table[currentIndex].key;
        if (collisionKey === key) {
          // There is a collision, so separate the chains
          const newTable = {};
          for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(this.table)) {
            if (k !== currentIndex) {
              newTable[k] = v;
            }
          }
          this.table = newTable;
          break;
        }
        currentIndex++;
      }
    }
    this.table[index].push({ key, value });
  }

  get(key) {
    const index = this.hashCode(key);
    return this.table[index];
  }

  hashCode(key) {
    // A simple hash function that returns a random number between 0 and the length of the table
    return Math.floor(Math.random() * this.table.length);
  }
}
```
In this modified version of the `HashTable` class, we check for collisions when inserting new key-value pairs into the table. If there is a collision, we separate the chains by creating a new hash table with the same size as the original one and copy all the elements from the original table to the new one. This ensures that each key has its own unique index in the table and avoids collisions.

With this implementation, you can store multiple values for a single key using separate chaining. When retrieving a value for a given key, you can iterate through all the elements at the same index to find the matching key-value pair.

