In CSS, there are several methods for selecting specific HTML elements. These include:

1. Unique Identifier (id): The id attribute assigns a unique identifier to an HTML element, allowing it to be selected using its ID in the CSS file. For example, if we have an HTML element with the ID "myID," we can select it in CSS by using the selector "#myID."
```css
#myID {
  color: red;
}
```
2. Class: The class attribute assigns a class name to an HTML element, allowing it to be selected using its class in the CSS file. For example, if we have an HTML element with the class "myClass," we can select it in CSS by using the selector ".myClass."
```css
.myClass {
  background-color: blue;
}
```
3. Attribute Selector: The attribute selector allows us to select elements based on their attributes, such as a specific value or presence of an attribute. For example, if we have an HTML element with the attribute "data-type" set to "button," we can select it in CSS by using the selector "[data-type='button']."
```css
[data-type="button"] {
  border: 1px solid black;
}
```
4. Child Selector: The child selector allows us to select elements based on their position within a container element. For example, if we have an HTML element with the class "container" and it contains an element with the class "child," we can select the ".child" element within the ".container" element in CSS by using the selector ".container > .child."
```css
.container > .child {
  margin: 20px;
}
```
5. Descendant Selector: The descendant selector allows us to select elements based on their position relative to another element. For example, if we have an HTML element with the class "parent" and it contains an element with the class "child," we can select the ".child" element within the ".parent" element in CSS by using the selector ".parent .child."
```css
.parent .child {
  padding: 10px;
}
```
6. Adjacent Sibling Selector: The adjacent sibling selector allows us to select elements that are immediately preceded by another element. For example, if we have an HTML element with the class "previous" and it is immediately followed by an element with the class "next," we can select the ".next" element in CSS by using the selector ".previous + .next."
```css
.previous + .next {
  margin-top: 20px;
}
```
7. General Sibling Selector: The general sibling selector allows us to select elements that are preceded by another element, but not necessarily immediately so. For example, if we have an HTML element with the class "previous" and it is followed by one or more elements with the class "next," we can select all of the ".next" elements in CSS by using the selector ".previous ~ .next."
```css
.previous ~ .next {
  margin-top: 20px;
}
```
These are some of the methods for selecting specific HTML elements in CSS. By combining these methods, we can select multiple elements of different types and apply a particular style to them. For example, if we want to select all ".child" elements within ".container" elements that also have the class "myClass," we can use the following selector:
```css
.container.myClass > .child {
  margin: 20px;
}
```
This will select all ".child" elements within ".container" elements with both classes "myClass" and apply the style of setting a margin of 20 pixels to them.

