Here is a possible implementation of the `KeyValueStore` class:
```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <stdexcept>

class KeyValueStore {
public:
    void insert(const Key& key, const Value& value) {
        // If the key already exists, update its associated value.
        if (store_.find(key) != store_.end()) {
            store_[key] = value;
        } else {
            // Otherwise, insert a new key-value pair into the store.
            store_.emplace(key, value);
        }
    }

    const Value& getValue(const Key& key) const {
        auto it = store_.find(key);
        if (it == store_.end()) {
            throw std::out_of_range("Key not found in the store");
        }
        return it->second;
    }

private:
    // Use an unordered map to store key-value pairs.
    std::unordered_map<Key, Value> store_;
};
```
Explanation:

The `KeyValueStore` class provides two methods: `insert()` and `getValue()`.

* The `insert()` method takes a `const Key&` reference and a `const Value&` reference as input. It inserts the key-value pair into the store, updating the associated value if the key already exists.
* The `getValue()` method takes a `const Key&` reference as input and returns a `const Value&` reference to the corresponding value in the store. If the key does not exist in the store, an exception is thrown.

The class uses an `std::unordered_map<Key, Value>` to store key-value pairs. The `std::unordered_map` provides efficient insertion, deletion, and retrieval of elements based on their hash values.

Note that the `getValue()` method is declared as `const`, meaning it does not modify the contents of the store. This is important for ensuring that the store remains in a consistent state and can be used safely across multiple threads.

