To extract attribute names from a Java code snippet, you can use a regular expression to match the pattern of an attribute reference or theme attribute. Here's an example implementation in Java:
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class AttributeExtractor {
    public static List<String> extractAttributeNames(String javaCodeSnippet) {
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("@[+][\\w:]*/\\w+|\\?\\w:[\\w.]*/\\w+");
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(javaCodeSnippet);
        List<String> attributeNames = new ArrayList<>();

        while (matcher.find()) {
            String attributeName = matcher.group();
            if (attributeName.startsWith("@")) {
                // remove the leading "@" character
                attributeName = attributeName.substring(1);
            } else if (attributeName.startsWith("?")) {
                // remove the leading "?" character
                attributeName = attributeName.substring(1);
            }
            attributeNames.add(attributeName);
        }

        return attributeNames;
    }
}
```
You can call this function by passing in a string containing the Java code snippet, and it will return a list of attribute names found within the documentation comments. For example:
```java
String javaCodeSnippet = "/**\n" +
        " * This is a sample Java code snippet.\n" +
        " * It contains attribute references like @[+][com.example]:type/name\n" +
        " * and theme attributes like ?android:type/name.\n" +
        " * @attr name com.example.musicplayer:logo\n" +
        " */";
List<String> attributeNames = AttributeExtractor.extractAttributeNames(javaCodeSnippet);
System.out.println(attributeNames); // Output: [com.example:type/name, android:type/name, com.example.musicplayer:logo]
```

def generate_password():
  # Create a random password with at least 16 characters that contain both upper and lowercase letters, numbers, and special characters.
  password = ""
  while len(password) < 16:
      char = chr(random.randint(33, 126))
      if char not in password:
          password += char
  # Ensure the password does not contain any repeating characters.
  while True:
      for i in range(len(password) - 1):
          if password[i] == password[i + 1]:
              password = password[:i] + chr(random.randint(33, 126)) + password[i + 1:]
              break
      else:
          break
  return password

