Here is a sample JavaScript code that should achieve the desired behavior:
```
// Get all links in the unordered list
const links = document.querySelectorAll('ul li a');

// Add click event listener to each link
links.forEach((link) => {
  link.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
    // Prevent default behavior of clicking on the link
    event.preventDefault();

    // Get the text content of the clicked link
    const linkText = link.textContent;

    // Open a new tab with the URL hash set to the name of the clicked link
    window.open(`#${linkText}`, '_blank');
  });
});

// Handle manual changes to the URL hash
window.addEventListener('hashchange', (event) => {
  const hash = event.target.hash;

  // Check if the hash is one of the links in the unordered list
  if (links.includes(hash)) {
    // Open a new tab with the URL hash set to the name of the clicked link
    window.open(`#${hash}`, '_blank');
  } else {
    // Do nothing, as the hash is not one of the links in the unordered list
  }
});
```
This code uses the `querySelectorAll()` method to get all links in the unordered list. It then adds a click event listener to each link using the `forEach()` method. When a link is clicked, it prevents the default behavior of clicking on the link and opens a new tab with the URL hash set to the name of the clicked link.

The code also handles manual changes to the URL hash by adding an event listener to the `hashchange` event. When the hash part of the URL changes, it checks if the hash is one of the links in the unordered list and opens a new tab with the URL hash set to the name of the clicked link if it is. If the hash is not one of the links in the unordered list, nothing happens.

