```
// Use a sieve of eratosthenes to find all prime numbers up to n
public static List<Integer> getPrimeNumbers(int n) {
    boolean[] isComposite = new boolean[n+1]; // mark as composite
    for (int i = 2; i*i <= n; i++) {
        if (!isComposite[i]) {
            for (int j = i*i; j <= n; j += i) {
                isComposite[j] = true;
            }
        }
    }
    List<Integer> primeNumbers = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        if (!isComposite[i]) {
            primeNumbers.add(i);
        }
    }
    return primeNumbers;
}
```
This implementation uses the sieve of eratosthenes algorithm to find all prime numbers up to `n`. It's a more efficient method than iterating through every number and checking if it's divisible by any other number.

Also, as you mentioned, using parameterized queries can help prevent SQL injection attacks. Here's an example of how to use a parameterized query in Java:
```
// Using a parameterized query to insert data into the database
public void insertData(String tableName, String columnNames, List<Object> values) {
    try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:sqlserver://localhost:1433;databaseName=mydb", "username", "password")) {
        PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement("INSERT INTO ? VALUES(?, ?)");
        ps.setString(1, tableName);
        for (int i = 0; i < columnNames.length(); i++) {
            ps.setObject(i + 2, values.get(i));
        }
        int rowsInserted = ps.executeUpdate();
        System.out.println("Rows inserted: " + rowsInserted);
    } catch (SQLException e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}
```
In this example, the `insertData` method takes in a `tableName`, a `columnNames` string array, and a `values` list of objects to be inserted into the database. It uses a parameterized query to insert the data into the specified table. This approach helps prevent SQL injection attacks by avoiding the need for manual escaping of special characters.

