You are correct, my previous response lacked input sanitization, which can make the application vulnerable to SQL injection attacks. Additionally, using prepared statements instead of direct string concatenation in SQL queries can improve both efficiency and security. Prepared statements can help protect against SQL injection by allowing the database to handle any necessary escaping or formatting, rather than relying on the developer to do so.

Here is an updated version of the code that incorporates input sanitization and prepared statements:
```php
<?php

// Connect to the database
$conn = new mysqli($servername, $username, $password, $dbname);

// Check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}

// Sanitize input
$firstName = $conn->real_escape_string($_POST['firstName']);
$lastName = $conn->real_escape_string($_POST['lastName']);
$email = $conn->real_escape_string($_POST['email']);

// Prepare the SQL statement
$sql = "INSERT INTO users (first_name, last_name, email) VALUES (?, ?, ?)";
$stmt = $conn->prepare($sql);

// Bind the input to the prepared statement
$stmt->bind_param("sss", $firstName, $lastName, $email);

// Execute the prepared statement
$stmt->execute();

// Close the connection
$conn->close();
```
This updated code uses `mysqli::real_escape_string()` to sanitize input and `mysqli::prepare()` to prepare the SQL statement. The input is then bound to the prepared statement using `mysqli::bind_param()`. Finally, the prepared statement is executed using `mysqli::execute()`.

