This problem can be solved using a combination of DFS and a hash table. The basic idea is to use DFS to traverse the binary search tree, while keeping track of the current depth of each node. If the current node is at an even depth, we add its value to a hash table. We then use this hash table to compute the cumulative sum of all values within the specified range.

Here's an example implementation in JavaScript:
```
function findSum(root, low, high) {
  const hash = new Map();

  function dfs(node, depth) {
    if (node == null) return;

    // Add current node to hash table if at even depth
    if (depth % 2 === 0) {
      hash.set(node.val, true);
    }

    // Recursively traverse left and right subtrees
    dfs(node.left, depth + 1);
    dfs(node.right, depth + 1);
  }

  // Initialize hash table with empty values for each node value
  const values = new Map();
  values.set(null, null);
  root.val = null;

  // Traverse binary search tree and fill hash table with node values
  dfs(root, 0);

  // Compute cumulative sum within specified range
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = low; i <= high; i++) {
    if (hash.has(i)) {
      sum += i;
    }
  }

  return sum;
}
```
In this implementation, we use a hash table (`Map`) to keep track of the values of nodes at even depths in the binary search tree. We fill this hash table with the values of all nodes at an even depth during DFS traversal. Then, we compute the cumulative sum within the specified range by iterating through the values in the hash table and adding them up.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the binary search tree. The space complexity is O(n) as well, since we need to store all node values in the hash table. This solution works under the assumption that the input binary search tree is valid and follows the constraints specified in the problem statement.

