Certainly! Here's an outline of how to construct a binary search tree in Java:

1. Define a `Node` class to represent each node in the tree. The `Node` class should have properties for the value being stored (in this case, an integer), the left child node, and the right child node.
2. Define a `BinarySearchTree` class that represents the entire binary search tree. This class should have a root property to keep track of the root node of the tree, as well as methods for adding new nodes, removing nodes, and traversing the tree.
3. To add a new node to the tree, you can use a recursive approach by first checking if the value being added is less than or greater than the root node's value. If it's less, you recursively call `addNode` on the left child node; if it's greater, you recursively call `addNode` on the right child node. If the value being added is equal to the root node's value, you simply return the current root node.
4. To remove a node from the tree, you can use a similar recursive approach by first checking if the value being removed is less than or greater than the root node's value. If it's less, you recursively call `removeNode` on the left child node; if it's greater, you recursively call `removeNode` on the right child node. If the value being removed is equal to the root node's value, you simply set the current root node to either the left or right child node (depending on which one still exists), and return the new root node.
5. To traverse the tree in order, you can use a recursive approach by traversing each subtree recursively, starting with the left child node if it exists, then the root node, and finally the right child node if it exists. You can do this using a method such as `inorderTraversal`, which takes a `Node` object as an argument and returns an array of integers representing the values in order.

Here's some sample code to illustrate how to construct a binary search tree, add nodes, remove nodes, and traverse the tree:
```
public class Node {
    int value;
    Node leftChild;
    Node rightChild;

    public Node(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }
}

public class BinarySearchTree {
    private Node root;

    public BinarySearchTree() {
        root = null;
    }

    public void addNode(int value) {
        if (root == null) {
            root = new Node(value);
        } else {
            addNodeRecursive(root, value);
        }
    }

    private void addNodeRecursive(Node node, int value) {
        if (node.value < value) {
            if (node.leftChild == null) {
                node.leftChild = new Node(value);
            } else {
                addNodeRecursive(node.leftChild, value);
            }
        } else if (node.value > value) {
            if (node.rightChild == null) {
                node.rightChild = new Node(value);
            } else {
                addNodeRecursive(node.rightChild, value);
            }
        }
    }

    public void removeNode(int value) {
        if (root != null && root.value == value) {
            root = null;
        } else {
            removeNodeRecursive(root, value);
        }
    }

    private void removeNodeRecursive(Node node, int value) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }
        if (value < node.value) {
            removeNodeRecursive(node.leftChild, value);
        } else if (value > node.value) {
            removeNodeRecursive(node.rightChild, value);
        } else {
            // If the value is found in a child node, remove it and update the parent's left/right child property
            Node child = (value < node.value) ? node.leftChild : node.rightChild;
            if (child != null && child.value == value) {
                node.value = value;
                node.leftChild = node.rightChild = null;
            }
        }
    }

    public int[] inorderTraversal() {
        return inorderTraversal(root);
    }

    private int[] inorderTraversal(Node node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return new int[0];
        }
        int[] left = inorderTraversal(node.leftChild);
        int[] right = inorderTraversal(node.rightChild);
        int[] result = new int[left.length + right.length + 1];
        System.arraycopy(left, 0, result, 0, left.length);
        result[left.length] = node.value;
        System.arraycopy(right, 0, result, left.length + 1, right.length);
        return result;
    }
}
```
You can use this `BinarySearchTree` class in the following way:
```
BinarySearchTree tree = new BinarySearchTree();
tree.addNode(5);
tree.addNode(2);
tree.addNode(8);
tree.addNode(3);
tree.addNode(7);
tree.removeNode(7);
int[] result = tree.inorderTraversal();
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result)); // [2, 3, 5, 8]
```

