def find_median(my_list):
    if len(my_list) == 1:
        return my_list[0]

    left = 0
    right = len(my_list) - 1

    while left <= right:
        mid = (left + right) // 2
        if my_list[mid] > my_list[right]:
            left = mid + 1
        elif my_list[mid] < my_list[left]:
            right = mid - 1
        else:
            return my_list[mid]

    return None
```
This version of the code uses a binary search to find the median element in the list. It starts by finding the middle index of the list and then checks if the element at that index is greater than or less than the element at the rightmost index. If it is greater, it sets the left index to the midpoint plus 1, otherwise it sets the right index to the midpoint minus 1. It continues this process until it finds an element that is neither greater nor less than its neighboring elements. Once it has found such an element, it returns it as the median.

The time complexity of this version of the code is O(log n) because it uses a binary search to find the median element in the list, which takes logarithmic time in the worst case. The space complexity is also O(1) because it only uses a constant amount of memory regardless of the size of the input list.

