The `sigprocmask()` function is used to manipulate the set of signals that are blocked or ignored by the process. The function takes three arguments: `how`, which specifies how the signal mask should be modified; `set`, which specifies the new signal mask; and `oldset`, which returns the old signal mask.

Here's an example of how to use `sigprocmask()` to block SIGUSR1 for a few seconds:
```c
#include <signal.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    // Block SIGUSR1
    sigset_t set;
    sigemptyset(&set);
    sigaddset(&set, SIGUSR1);
    sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, &set, NULL);

    // Do some work for a few seconds
    sleep(5);

    // Unblock SIGUSR1 and handle it if it was received while it was blocked
    sigprocmask(SIG_UNBLOCK, &set, NULL);
    if (sigismember(&set, SIGUSR1)) {
        printf("SIGUSR1 was received\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we first create an empty signal set `set` using `sigemptyset()`. We then add SIGUSR1 to the set using `sigaddset(&set, SIGUSR1)`. This sets up a signal mask that blocks all signals except for SIGUSR1.

Next, we use `sigprocmask()` with `SIG_BLOCK` as the first argument to block the signals in the set. The second argument is the new signal mask, and the third argument is the old signal mask. Since we don't need the old signal mask, we pass NULL as the third argument.

We then do some work for a few seconds using `sleep(5)`. During this time, SIGUSR1 will be blocked from being received by the process.

After sleeping for a few seconds, we unblock SIGUSR1 using `sigprocmask()` with `SIG_UNBLOCK` as the first argument. This sets up a new signal mask that includes all signals except for SIGUSR1.

Finally, we check if SIGUSR1 was received while it was blocked using `sigismember(&set, SIGUSR1)`. If it was, we print a message indicating that SIGUSR1 was received.

Note that the `sigprocmask()` function is typically used to block or unblock signals that are not handled by the process. If you want to handle signals in your program, you should use the `signal()` function instead.

