    if (n <= 1) {
        return 1;
    }

    // Recursive case: Calculate the factorial of n-1 and multiply it by n.
    return n * calculateFactorial(n-1);
}

Here's an example of how you might add comments to explain the logic and steps of a simple sorting algorithm:
```
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  // The bubble sort algorithm is a simple sorting algorithm that works by repeatedly
  // iterating through the array, comparing adjacent elements, and swapping them if
  // they are in the wrong order. This process continues until no more swaps can be
  // made, indicating that the array is sorted.

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
    // On each iteration of the outer loop, we compare adjacent elements in the
    // array and swap them if they are in the wrong order. We start at the first
    // element and continue to the last element, working our way through the array.

    for (let j = 0; j < arr.length - i - 1; j++) {
      // On each iteration of the inner loop, we compare the current element with its
      // neighbor and swap them if they are in the wrong order. We start at the first
      // element and continue to the last element, working our way through the array.

      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        // If the current element is greater than its neighbor, we swap them.
        [arr[j], arr[j + 1]] = [arr[j + 1], arr[j]];
      }
    }
  }

  return arr;
}
```
In this example, the comments explain the logic and steps of the algorithm in a way that makes it easier to understand for someone reading the code. The comments describe what the algorithm is doing on each iteration of the outer loop and the inner loop, and why certain decisions are being made. This can help make the code more readable and easier to maintain.

