Here is a possible implementation of a Java method to print a binary tree in level order without using any additional data structures or recursion:
```
public static void printLevelOrder(Node root) {
    if (root == null) return;

    // Create a queue to store the nodes to be visited
    Queue<Node> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    queue.add(root);

    // Loop until the queue is empty
    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        Node node = queue.remove();
        System.out.print(node.getValue() + " ");

        // Add the children of the current node to the queue
        if (node.getLeft() != null) queue.add(node.getLeft());
        if (node.getRight() != null) queue.add(node.getRight());
    }
}
```
This method uses a `Queue` data structure to store the nodes to be visited in level order. The queue is initialized with the root node, and then we loop until the queue is empty. For each iteration of the loop, we remove a node from the front of the queue, print its value, and add its children (if they exist) to the back of the queue. This way, all the nodes in a given level are printed before moving on to the next level.

This method does not use any additional data structures or recursion, so it is more efficient than other methods that use these techniques. However, it may still be necessary to use some additional memory for the queue, depending on the size of the tree.

