Here is an example of a JavaScript function that computes the minimal number of rotations required to morph one linked list into a separate, unique one:
```
function rotateLists(list1, list2) {
  // Initialize variables for tracking the rotation sequence and the number of rotations needed
  let rotationSequence = [];
  let numRotations = 0;

  // Check if the linked lists are both singly linked or doubly linked
  if (list1.length === list2.length && list1[0] === list2[0]) {
    return null;
  } else if (list1.length > list2.length) {
    // The longer list is the one that will be rotated to match the shorter list
    let tempList = list1;
    list1 = list2;
    list2 = tempList;
  }

  // Check if the linked lists contain repeated elements
  const repeatedElements = {};
  for (let i = 0; i < list1.length; i++) {
    if (repeatedElements[list1[i]]) {
      return null;
    } else {
      repeatedElements[list1[i]] = true;
    }
  }

  // Find the index of the first element that is not in the shorter list
  let firstIndex = -1;
  for (let i = 0; i < list2.length; i++) {
    if (!list1.includes(list2[i])) {
      firstIndex = i;
      break;
    }
  }

  // If the shorter list is not a subset of the longer list, return null
  if (firstIndex === -1) {
    return null;
  }

  // Compute the minimal number of rotations needed to morph the shorter list into the longer list
  let currentIndex = firstIndex;
  while (currentIndex < list2.length) {
    rotationSequence.push([list2[currentIndex], list1[currentIndex]]);
    numRotations++;
    currentIndex++;
  }

  // Return the rotation sequence and the number of rotations needed
  return {rotationSequence, numRotations};
}
```
This function takes two linked lists as input, `list1` and `list2`, and computes the minimal number of rotations needed to morph `list1` into a separate, unique list. The function first checks if the two lists are both singly linked or doubly linked, and returns null if they are not. It then checks if the shorter list is a subset of the longer list, and returns null if it is not. If the shorter list is not a subset of the longer list, the function computes the minimal number of rotations needed to morph the shorter list into the longer list by iterating through each element in the shorter list and finding its corresponding index in the longer list. The function then adds the rotation sequence and the number of rotations needed to an object and returns it.

Here is an example usage of the function:
```
