Here is an example of how you can set up an AWS Lambda function that sends an HTTP request to an API Gateway:
```
// Import the necessary modules
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const https = require('https');

// Set up the AWS Lambda function
exports.handler = async (event, context) => {
  // Define the parameters for the HTTP request
  const method = 'GET';
  const url = 'https://api-gateway-url/path';
  const headers = {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
  };

  // Make the HTTP request using the AWS Lambda function's credentials
  const response = await https.request(method, url, headers, context.identity);

  // Return the response from the API Gateway
  return { statusCode: response.statusCode, body: JSON.stringify(response.body) };
};
```
This code defines an AWS Lambda function that sends an HTTP GET request to an API Gateway using the `https` module and returns the response as a JSON object. The `context.identity` parameter is used to pass in the credentials for the AWS Lambda function, which are used to make the HTTP request.

You can deploy this code as a new version of your existing AWS Lambda function or create a new AWS Lambda function using the AWS Management Console or the AWS CLI. Once deployed, you can test the function by calling it with an API Gateway endpoint URL and passing in any necessary parameters.

