  // Loop through all the child nodes of the current element
  for (let i = 0; i < this.children.length; i++) {
    // Get the current child node
    let child = this.children[i];

    // If the child node matches the selector, add it to the NodeList
    if (child.matches(selector)) {
      nodeList.push(child);
    }
  }

  return nodeList;
}
```
The comments in this version of the code provide additional context and explain the purpose of each line of code, making it easier for developers to understand and maintain the code.

Here is an example implementation of a Q-learning agent for playing Blackjack:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

// Define the action space and observation space
const int ACTION_SPACE = 2; // hit or stay
const int OBSERVATION_SPACE = 3; // current sum, dealer's showing card, usable ace

// Define the Q-values for each state-action pair
vector<vector<float>> qValues(OBSERVATION_SPACE, vector<float>(ACTION_SPACE));

// Initialize Q-values with random values
for (int s = 0; s < OBSERVATION_SPACE; s++) {
    for (int a = 0; a < ACTION_SPACE; a++) {
        qValues[s][a] = (float)rand() / RAND_MAX;
    }
}

// Define the exploration rate and decay factor
const float EXPLORATION_RATE = 0.1;
const float DECAY_FACTOR = 0.99;

// Function to update Q-values based on rewards and next state
void updateQValues(int state, int action, int reward, int nextState) {
    // Compute the TD error
    float tdError = reward + GAMMA * qValues[nextState][0] - qValues[state][action];

    // Update Q-values using TD error and exploration rate
    for (int s = 0; s < OBSERVATION_SPACE; s++) {
        for (int a = 0; a < ACTION_SPACE; a++) {
            qValues[s][a] += EXPLORATION_RATE * tdError;
        }
    }
}

// Function to select an action based on the current state and Q-values
int selectAction(int state) {
    // Compute the best Q-value for the current state
    float maxQValue = -1e9;
    int bestAction = 0;
    for (int a = 0; a < ACTION_SPACE; a++) {
        if (qValues[state][a] > maxQValue) {
            maxQValue = qValues[state][a];
            bestAction = a;
        }
    }

    // Decay the Q-values and return the selected action
    for (int s = 0; s < OBSERVATION_SPACE; s++) {
        for (int a = 0; a < ACTION_SPACE; a++) {
            qValues[s][a] *= DECAY_FACTOR;
        }
    }

    return bestAction;
}

// Function to train the agent using Q-learning
void trainAgent() {
    // Initialize the environment and the agent
    BlackJackCPP env;
    int state = env.reset();

    // Update Q-values based on rewards and next states until the episode ends
    while (!env.isEnd()) {
        int action = selectAction(state);
        int reward = env.step(action);
        int nextState = env.getState();

        updateQValues(state, action, reward, nextState);

        state = nextState;
    }
}

// Function to demonstrate the performance of the Q-learning agent
void demoAgent() {
    // Initialize the environment and the agent
    BlackJackCPP env;
    int state = env.reset();

    // Update Q-values based on rewards and next states until the episode ends
    while (!env.isEnd()) {
        int action = selectAction(state);
        int reward = env.step(action);
        int nextState = env.getState();

        updateQValues(state, action, reward, nextState);

        state = nextState;
    }

    // Print the final Q-values
    for (int s = 0; s < OBSERVATION_SPACE; s++) {
        cout << "Final Q-values: " << qValues[s][0] << ", " << qValues[s][1] << endl;
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `BlackJackCPP` class represents the Blackjack environment, and the `trainAgent()` function trains the Q-learning agent using the provided environment. The `selectAction()` function selects an action based on the current state and Q-values, while the `updateQValues()` function updates the Q-values for each state-action pair. The `demoAgent()` function demonstrates the performance of the Q-learning agent by printing the final Q-values.

To train the agent using Q-learning, we can call the `trainAgent()` function multiple times, each time with a different initial state and updated Q-values based on the rewards obtained from the environment. The agent will learn to play Blackjack optimally by updating its Q-values based on the rewards obtained from the environment.

To demonstrate the performance of the Q-learning agent, we can call the `demoAgent()` function once with a specific initial state and updated Q-values. This will allow us to see the final Q-values learned by the agent during training.

