def longest_increasing_subsequence(nums):
    dp = [1] * len(nums)
    for i in range(1, len(nums)):
        for j in range(i):
            if nums[i] > nums[j]:
                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)
    return max(dp)
```
This code uses a dynamic programming approach to solve the problem. It starts by initializing a list `dp` of the same length as the input list, filled with 1s. The elements of `dp` represent the length of the longest increasing subsequence ending at each index in the input list.

The code then iterates through the input list starting from the second element and iteratively updates the values in `dp` based on the current element and its previous elements. For each element `nums[i]`, it iterates through all the previous elements `nums[j]` and checks if `nums[i] > nums[j]`. If so, it updates `dp[i]` to the maximum of `dp[i]` and `dp[j] + 1`. This means that if the current element is greater than the previous element, we can extend the longest increasing subsequence ending at the previous element by including the current element.

The code then returns the maximum value in `dp` as the result. The time complexity of this solution is O(n^2), where n is the length of the input list.

