This problem can be solved using a recursive approach. The basic idea is to traverse the tree, and at each node that is at the given depth, create a new node with the given value as its left child and right child. If the current node has no children, then add it as the root of the new tree.

Here's an example implementation in Java:
```java
class Solution {
    public Node addOneRow(Node root, int val, int depth) {
        if (root == null || depth < 1) return root;

        // create a new node with the given value as its left child and right child
        Node newRoot = new Node(val);
        newRoot.left = root;
        newRoot.right = new Node(val);

        // traverse the tree, adding new nodes at the correct depth
        return addOneRow(newRoot, val, depth - 1);
    }

    private Node addOneRow(Node node, int val, int depth) {
        if (node == null || depth < 1) return node;

        // create a new node with the given value as its left child and right child
        Node newLeft = new Node(val);
        Node newRight = new Node(val);

        // add the new nodes to the current node
        node.left = newLeft;
        node.right = newRight;

        // recursively traverse the tree, adding new nodes at the correct depth
        addOneRow(node.left, val, depth - 1);
        addOneRow(node.right, val, depth - 1);

        return node;
    }
}
```
This solution has a time complexity of O(n) and space complexity of O(1), where n is the number of nodes in the tree. The time complexity is due to the fact that we need to traverse the entire tree to add new nodes at the correct depth. The space complexity is due to the fact that we only need to store a few nodes in memory at any given time, as we are not using any additional data structures to keep track of the nodes.

In this solution, we first check if the root node is null or if the depth is less than 1. If so, we return the original tree. Otherwise, we create a new node with the given value as its left child and right child, and add it to the current node as its left and right children, respectively. We then recursively traverse the tree, adding new nodes at the correct depth and updating the current node's left and right children accordingly.

The addOneRow method has a time complexity of O(n) due to the fact that we need to traverse the entire tree to add new nodes at the correct depth. The space complexity is O(1) because we are not using any additional data structures to keep track of the nodes.

Overall, this solution should work for all test cases and has a good balance between time and space complexity.

