```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Country Visits</title>
    <style>
        table {
            border-collapse: collapse;
            width: 100%;
        }

        th, td {
            padding: 8px;
            text-align: left;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
        }

        tr:nth-child(even) {background-color: #f2f2f2;}
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Country Visits</h1>
    <input type="text" id="search-input">
    <table id="visit-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th><button class="sort" data-sort="country">Country</button></th>
                <th><button class="sort" data-sort="visitors">Visitors</button></th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="visit-table-body"></tbody>
    </table>
    <script>
        // JSON data from external file
        const visitData = {
            "countries": [
                { "country": "United States", "visitors": 206 },
                { "country": "China", "visitors": 61 },
                { "country": "Spain", "visitors": 82 },
                { "country": "United Kingdom", "visitors": 37 },
                { "country": "Germany", "visitors": 37 },
                { "country": "France", "visitors": 86 },
                { "country": "Italy", "visitors": 58 },
                { "country": "Turkey", "visitors": 39 },
                { "country": "Japan", "visitors": 28 },
                { "country": "Russia", "visitors": 28 }
            ]
        };

        // Function to sort table by column
        function sortTable(column) {
            const table = document.getElementById("visit-table");
            const tbody = table.querySelector("tbody");
            const rows = Array.from(tbody.children);
            const sortedRows = rows.sort((a, b) => a.dataset[column] - b.dataset[column]);

            for (const row of sortedRows) {
                tbody.appendChild(row);
            }
        }

        // Function to filter table by search input
        function filterTable() {
            const input = document.getElementById("search-input");
            const table = document.getElementById("visit-table");
            const tbody = table.querySelector("tbody");
            const rows = Array.from(tbody.children);

            const searchValue = input.value.toLowerCase();
            if (searchValue === "") {
                return;
            }

            const filteredRows = rows.filter((row) => row.dataset.country.toLowerCase().includes(searchValue));
            tbody.innerHTML = "";

            for (const row of filteredRows) {
                tbody.appendChild(row);
            }
        }

        // Initialize table sort and filter functions
        document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () => {
            const buttons = document.querySelectorAll(".sort");
            const input = document.getElementById("search-input");

            for (const button of buttons) {
                button.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
                    sortTable(button.dataset.sort);
                });
            }

            input.addEventListener("keyup", filterTable);
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```
This code creates an HTML table with a header row and a tbody element that will be populated with the data from the JSON file. The script also includes two functions: `sortTable()` to sort the table by column, and `filterTable()` to filter the table based on search input.

The `DOMContentLoaded` event listener is used to initialize the table sort and filter functions when the page has finished loading. The buttons with class `.sort` are added as event listeners that call the `sortTable()` function when clicked, while the input element with id `search-input` is added as an event listener for the `keyup` event that calls the `filterTable()` function every time a key is pressed or released.

The `sortTable()` function uses the `Array.from()` method to convert the table body into an array of rows, and then sorts the array using the `sort()` method with a custom sort function that compares the values in the specified column. The sorted rows are then appended to the table body.

The `filterTable()` function uses the `Array.prototype.filter()` method to filter out any rows from the table body that do not contain the search value in their country name field. It also updates the inner HTML of the table body with the filtered rows.

