Here is an example implementation of a `Stack` class in Java:
```
public class Stack<T> {
    private List<T> data;

    public Stack() {
        this.data = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void push(T item) {
        data.add(item);
    }

    public T pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }

        return data.remove(data.size() - 1);
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return data.isEmpty();
    }
}
```
This implementation uses an `ArrayList` to store the items in the stack, and provides methods for pushing and popping items from the stack. The `push` method adds a new item to the top of the stack, while the `pop` method removes the last item from the stack and returns it. The `isEmpty` method checks if the stack is empty or not.

