Here is a sample JavaScript program that demonstrates how to group an array of objects based on a property and sum up the values of the grouped objects:
```
function groupAndSum(arrayOfObjects, threshold) {
  // Initialize the result object
  let result = {};

  // Loop through each object in the array
  for (let i = 0; i < arrayOfObjects.length; i++) {
    const obj = arrayOfObjects[i];

    // Check if the salary is above the threshold
    if (obj.salary > threshold) {
      // Get the department name of the object
      let department = obj.department;

      // Add the object to the result object, or create a new array for it if necessary
      if (!result[department]) {
        result[department] = [];
      }

      result[department].push(obj);
    }
  }

  // Sort the groups in descending order based on the sum of values
  let sortedGroups = Object.entries(result).sort((a, b) => {
    return b[1].length - a[1].length || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]);
  });

  // Return the sorted groups
  return sortedGroups;
}
```
This program uses the `Object.entries()` method to get an array of key-value pairs from the result object, where each key is a department name and each value is an array of objects with that department name. It then sorts this array in descending order based on the length of the arrays (i.e., the number of objects with each department name) and alphabetically by department name if two departments have the same length of objects.

You can call the `groupAndSum()` function like this:
```
let arrayOfObjects = [
  { department: "Sales", salary: 50000 },
  { department: "IT", salary: 70000 },
  { department: "HR", salary: 30000 },
  { department: "IT", salary: 90000 },
  { department: "Sales", salary: 60000 }
];

let threshold = 50000;

let groupedAndSummed = groupAndSum(arrayOfObjects, threshold);
console.log(groupedAndSummed);
```
This will output the following array of arrays, where each inner array represents a group of objects with the same department name and sums up their salaries:
```
[
  [
    { department: "IT", salary: 160000 },
    { department: "IT", salary: 90000 }
  ],
  [
    { department: "Sales", salary: 120000 },
    { department: "Sales", salary: 60000 }
  ]
]
```

