Here is an example implementation of the `log_execution_time` decorator:
```python
import time
from functools import wraps

def log_execution_time(include_docstring=False):
    def decorator(func):
        @wraps(func)
        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
            start = time.perf_counter()
            result = func(*args, **kwargs)
            end = time.perf_counter()
            execution_time = end - start
            if include_docstring:
                print(func.__doc__ + f" executed in {execution_time} seconds")
            else:
                print(f"{func.__name__} executed in {execution_time} seconds")
            return result
        return wrapper
    return decorator
```
This decorator takes an optional boolean argument `include_docstring` with a default value of `False`. When set to `True`, the decorator will print the function's docstring along with the execution time. Otherwise, it will only print the name of the function and its execution time.

To use this decorator, simply define a function as usual, then apply the `@log_execution_time` decorator to it, like so:
```python
@log_execution_time(include_docstring=True)
def my_function(x, y):
    """My function docstring"""
    return x + y
```
This will print the function's docstring along with its execution time when the function is called. If `include_docstring` is set to `False`, only the function name and execution time will be printed.

Note that this decorator works for functions with any number of arguments and return values. It uses the `wraps` decorator from the `functools` module to preserve the original function's signature and docstring, so it can still be used in the same way as any other function.

