function validateFormData($formData, $validationRules) {
    $errors = [];

    foreach ($validationRules as $field => $rule) {
        if (isset($formData[$field])) {
            // Check for required fields
            if ($rule === 'required' && empty($formData[$field])) {
                $errors[$field] = "The {$field} field is required.";
            }

            // Check for integer values
            elseif ($rule === 'integer' && !is_int($formData[$field])) {
                $errors[$field] = "The {$field} field must be an integer.";
            }

            // Check for minimum and maximum lengths
            elseif (strpos($rule, 'min:') !== false) {
                $length = (int) substr($rule, 4);
                if (strlen($formData[$field]) < $length) {
                    $errors[$field] = "The {$field} field must be at least {$length} characters.";
                }
            } elseif (strpos($rule, 'max:') !== false) {
                $length = (int) substr($rule, 4);
                if (strlen($formData[$field]) > $length) {
                    $errors[$field] = "The {$field} field must be no longer than {$length} characters.";
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return $errors;
}
```
This function takes two parameters: `$formData`, an associative array representing the form data to be validated, and `$validationRules`, an associative array representing the validation rules for each form field.

The function iterates over the `$validationRules` array using `foreach`, checking each field value against its corresponding rule. If a rule is violated, an error message is added to the `$errors` array with the field name as the key and the error message as the value. The function returns the `$errors` array at the end.

