Here is a function in JavaScript that sorts a list of strings in descending order based on their lengths:
```
function sortByLength(strList) {
  for (let i = 0; i < strList.length; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j < strList.length; j++) {
      if (strList[i].length > strList[j].length) {
        let temp = strList[i];
        strList[i] = strList[j];
        strList[j] = temp;
      }
    }
  }
  return strList;
}
```
The algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2), where n is the number of strings in the list, because it uses two nested loops to compare each string with every other string. The space complexity is O(1) because it only requires a constant amount of memory to store the sorted list of strings.

This algorithm works by first iterating through the list of strings and comparing each string with every other string using a nested loop. If two strings have different lengths, the longer string is swapped with the shorter string in the original position in the list. This process continues until no more swaps are needed. The final sorted list is returned.

This algorithm has the advantage of being easy to understand and implement, but it has a time complexity that can be slow for large lists of strings. However, for smaller lists, this algorithm is a good choice because it is simple and efficient.

