```
function verifyDistinct(arr) {
  // Initialize a hash set to store unique elements
  const set = new Set();

  // Loop through each element in the array
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    // If the current element is already in the hash set, return false
    if (set.has(arr[i])) {
      return false;
    }

    // Otherwise, add it to the hash set
    set.add(arr[i]);
  }

  // If we reach this point, all elements are distinct, so return true
  return true;
}
```
This function uses a hash set to keep track of the unique elements in the array, and returns `true` if all elements are distinct. It returns `false` if any duplicate element is found. The logic is as follows:

1. Initialize a new `Set` object to store unique elements.
2. Loop through each element in the input array.
3. If the current element is already in the hash set, return `false`. This indicates that there are duplicate elements in the array.
4. Otherwise, add the current element to the hash set.
5. If we reach the end of the loop without finding any duplicates, all elements are distinct, so return `true`.

The time complexity of this function is O(n), where n is the length of the input array, because at most one iteration of the loop will be performed for each element in the array. The space complexity is O(n) as well, because we need to store all the unique elements in the hash set.

It's important to note that this function assumes that the input array is not sorted and contains no duplicates. If the input array is sorted or contains duplicates, it may not work correctly.

