    int uppercaseCount = 0;
    int lowercaseCount = 0;
    int digitCount = 0;
    int specialCharacterCount = 0;

    for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
      if (Character.isUpperCase(c)) {
        uppercaseCount++;
      } else if (Character.isLowerCase(c)) {
        lowercaseCount++;
      } else if (Character.isDigit(c)) {
        digitCount++;
      } else if (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(c) && !Character.isWhitespace(c)) {
        specialCharacterCount++;
      }
    }

    System.out.println("Number of uppercase letters: " + uppercaseCount);
    System.out.println("Number of lowercase letters: " + lowercaseCount);
    System.out.println("Number of digits: " + digitCount);
    System.out.println("Number of special characters: " + specialCharacterCount);
  }
}
```
This program uses the `Scanner` class to read input from the user, and then removes all whitespace characters from the input string using a regular expression. The remaining characters are then counted as uppercase letters, lowercase letters, digits, or special characters using the `Character` class methods `isUpperCase()`, `isLowerCase()`, `isDigit()`, and `isLetterOrDigit()`. Finally, the program prints out the number of each type of character in the input string.

Note that this program only works for ASCII characters, so it may not work correctly for non-ASCII characters or languages with non-Latin alphabets. If you need to support more complex languages or character sets, you may need to use a different approach.

