Here's an example function that compares two strings using only bitwise operations:
```
function compareStrings(str1, str2) {
  let result = 0;
  const len1 = str1.length;
  const len2 = str2.length;
  const maxLen = Math.max(len1, len2);

  for (let i = 0; i < maxLen; i++) {
    const char1 = str1[i] || 'A';
    const char2 = str2[i] || 'A';
    result |= (char1 & char2) ^ ((char1 >> 5) & (char2 >> 5));
  }

  return result;
}
```
This function uses a bitwise OR (`|`) to compare each character in the two strings, and then shifts the characters by 5 bits to the right before comparing them. The `>>` operator is used to shift the bits to the right, while the `&` operator is used to perform a bitwise AND operation.

The function uses the `Math.max()` method to find the longer of the two strings and then iterates over each character in the shorter string, using the length of the longer string as the loop limit. If either of the characters being compared is undefined (i.e., out of bounds), it is treated as an 'A' for the purpose of comparison.

The time complexity of this function is O(n), where n is the length of the longer string, since it only iterates over each character in the shorter string once. The space complexity is constant, since the function does not use any additional memory beyond what is needed to store the two input strings and the result variable.

This function can be used to compare two strings and return a value indicating whether they are equal or not. It uses only bitwise operations and does not require any external libraries or functions.

