To add JavaScript validation, we can use the `onsubmit` event listener on the form element. When the form is submitted, the following JavaScript code will run:
```
const form = document.getElementById('registration-form');

form.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
  // Prevent the form from being submitted if there are any errors
  event.preventDefault();

  // Validate the name input field
  const nameInput = form.querySelector('input[name="name"]');
  if (!nameInput.value.match(/^[a-zA-Z\s]+$/)) {
    alert("Please enter a valid name with no numbers or special characters.");
    return;
  }

  // Validate the email input field
  const emailInput = form.querySelector('input[name="email"]');
  if (!emailInput.value) {
    alert("Please enter an email address.");
    return;
  }

  // Validate the password input field
  const passwordInput = form.querySelector('input[name="password"]');
  if (passwordInput.value.length < 8 || !passwordInput.value.match(/(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[0-9]).{8,}/)) {
    alert("Please enter a valid password with at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, and one number.");
    return;
  }

  // Validate the date of birth input field
  const dateOfBirthInput = form.querySelector('input[name="date_of_birth"]');
  if (!dateOfBirthInput.value.match(/^\d{1,2}\/\d{1,2}\/\d{4}$/)) {
    alert("Please enter a valid date of birth in the format MM/DD/YYYY.");
    return;
  }

  // If there are no errors, send the form data to the server using an AJAX call
  const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
  xhr.open('POST', '/register');
  xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
  xhr.send(JSON.stringify({ name: nameInput.value, email: emailInput.value, password: passwordInput.value, date_of_birth: dateOfBirthInput.value }));
});
```
In this code, we first get a reference to the form element using `document.getElementById('registration-form')`. We then add an event listener to the form's `submit` event using `addEventListener('submit', ...)`. When the form is submitted, the code inside the event handler will run.

Inside the event handler, we first call `event.preventDefault()` to prevent the form from being submitted if there are any errors. Then, we validate each input field using JavaScript regular expressions. If any of the inputs fail validation, an error message is displayed and the code returns early without sending the form data to the server.

If all inputs pass validation, we create a new `XMLHttpRequest` object and send the form data to the server using an AJAX call. The `Content-Type` header is set to `application/json`, which means that the form data will be sent as JSON data.

