Here is an example of how you can implement the `Cloner` class to meet the requirements:
```swift
class Cloner<T> {
    func clone(t: T) -> Self {
        let newObject = T()
        // copy all properties from t to newObject
        for prop in Mirror(reflecting: t).children {
            if let value = prop.value as? T {
                newObject[keyPath: prop.label!] = value
            }
        }
        return newObject as! Self
    }

    func getCloneFn<T>(t: T) -> () -> Self {
        // create a function that takes an object of type T and returns a clone of it
        let fn = { (object: T) in self.clone(object) }
        return fn
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `Cloner` class is generic on the type `T`. The `clone` method takes an object of type `T` and returns a deep copy of that object. The `getCloneFn` method takes an object of type `T` and returns a function that can be used to clone objects of the same type as the input object.

The `getCloneFn` method creates a new function that takes an object of type `T` and calls the `clone` method on it to create a deep copy of the input object. The returned function is of type `() -> Self`, which means it returns a clone of the input object of type `Self`.

To use this class, you can call the `getCloneFn` method with an object of any type and pass the result to another part of your code that needs to be able to clone objects of that type. For example:
```swift
let cloner = Cloner<MyObject>()
let myObject = MyObject()
let cloneFn = cloner.getCloneFn(myObject)
let clonedObject = cloneFn()
```
In this example, the `cloner` object is instantiated with a specific type (`MyObject`) and then the `getCloneFn` method is called with an instance of that type (`myObject`). The result of the `getCloneFn` method is stored in the `cloneFn` variable. Finally, the `cloneFn` function is called without any arguments to create a clone of the original object.

This implementation uses generics to make the `Cloner` class more flexible and reusable, as it can be used with any type that conforms to the `Equatable` protocol. The `clone` method creates a deep copy of the input object by iterating over its properties using the `Mirror` type, and the `getCloneFn` method returns a function that can be used to clone objects of the same type as the input object.

