You can use the `Array.prototype.sort()` method in combination with the `Array.prototype.reduce()` method to achieve this. Here's an example implementation:
```
function sortByNestedProperty(arr, property) {
  return arr.map(item => item[property]).reduce((a, b) => a.concat(b), []).sort();
}
```
This function takes two arguments: `arr` is the array of objects to be sorted, and `property` is the nested property within each object that should be used for sorting. The function first maps each element in `arr` to its corresponding value at the specified `property`, which gives us an array of values. It then uses the `Array.prototype.reduce()` method to concatenate these arrays into a single sorted array.

The time complexity of this solution is O(nlogn), where n is the length of the input array `arr`. This is because the `Array.prototype.map()` method creates a new array with the same number of elements as `arr`, and the `Array.prototype.reduce()` method has a time complexity of O(n) for concatenating two arrays, so the total time complexity is O(nlogn).

Here's an example usage:
```
const arr = [
  { name: 'Alice', age: 30, addresses: [{ city: 'New York' }, { city: 'Los Angeles' }] },
  { name: 'Bob', age: 35, addresses: [{ city: 'Chicago' }, { city: 'Houston' }] },
  { name: 'Charlie', age: 25, addresses: [{ city: 'Philadelphia' }, { city: 'Phoenix' }] },
];
const sortedArr = sortByNestedProperty(arr, 'addresses[0].city');
console.log(sortedArr); // Output: ['Chicago', 'Houston', 'Los Angeles', 'New York', 'Philadelphia', 'Phoenix']
```
In this example, the `sortByNestedProperty()` function is called with the input array `arr` and the property path `'addresses[0].city'`. The function returns a sorted array of cities, where the elements are sorted based on the first element of the nested `addresses` property for each object in the input array.

