Here's a possible implementation of a binary search algorithm with comments:
```
function binarySearch(arr, x) {
  // Set the low and high indices to the boundaries of the array
  let low = 0;
  let high = arr.length - 1;

  // While the low index is less than or equal to the high index
  while (low <= high) {
    // Calculate the middle index
    const mid = (low + high) >>> 1;

    // If the element at the middle index is less than the target value
    if (arr[mid] < x) {
      // Update the low index to the middle index plus one
      low = mid + 1;
    } else {
      // If the element at the middle index is greater than the target value
      // Update the high index to the middle index minus one
      high = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  // If the low index is less than the length of the array
  if (low < arr.length) {
    // Return the index of the first element greater than or equal to the target value
    return low;
  } else {
    // Otherwise, return -1 to indicate that the target value was not found
    return -1;
  }
}
```
The binary search algorithm is a efficient and reliable method for searching for a specific element in a sorted array. The algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the array into two halves until the target element is found or the low index exceeds the high index. The comments in the code explain the logic of the algorithm and how it is used to find the first element greater than or equal to the target value in a sorted array.

Here is a PHP function that can extract all terms commencing with a specified alphabetical character from a provided multi-dimensional array:
```php
function get_terms_by_initial($array, $initial) {
    $result = [];

    foreach ($array as $key => $value) {
        if (is_string($value)) {
            if (strpos($value, $initial) === 0) {
                $result[] = $value;
            }
        } elseif (is_array($value)) {
            $result = array_merge($result, get_terms_by_initial($value, $initial));
        }
    }

    return $result;
}
```
This function uses recursion to navigate through each depth of the array and checks if the current term starts with the specified initial character. If it does, it adds the term to the result array.

