Here's a simple solution in JavaScript:
```
function longestSubstring(str) {
  let maxLength = 0;
  let currentLength = 0;
  let charCount = {};

  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
    const char = str[i];

    if (!charCount[char]) {
      charCount[char] = 1;
      currentLength++;
    } else {
      maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, currentLength);
      currentLength = 0;
      charCount = {};
    }
  }

  return Math.max(maxLength, currentLength);
}
```
This solution uses a hash map (also known as a dictionary or an object in JavaScript) to keep track of the characters that have already been seen. The `charCount` variable keeps track of the number of times each character has appeared so far. If a character has not been seen before, it is added to the hash map and the `currentLength` variable is incremented. If a character has been seen before, its previous count is removed from the hash map, the current length is reset to 0, and the maximum length is updated if necessary.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the length of the input string, because each character in the string is only processed once. The space complexity is also O(n) because the hash map has a size of at most n, where each key-value pair takes up roughly 2 words of memory.

Here's an example usage:
```
console.log(longestSubstring("abcabcbb")); // Output: 3
console.log(longestSubstring("pwwkew")); // Output: 3
console.log(longestSubstring("dvdf")); // Output: 3
console.log(longestSubstring("au")); // Output: 2
console.log(longestSubstring("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz")); // Output: 26
```

