This problem can be solved using dynamic programming. The idea is to keep track of the number of ways to construct an array of length `ni` with product `ki`. We can use a two-dimensional dp table where `dp[i][j]` represents the number of ways to construct an array of length `i` with product `j`.

We can fill in the table by considering all possible values that can be inserted into the current position of the array. If the value is less than or equal to `ki`, we can add it to the product and recursively call the function for the remaining positions. If the value is greater than `ki`, we can skip it since it will not contribute to the final product.

The base case is when `i = 1` and `j = 0`. In this case, there is only one way to construct an array of length 1 with a product of 0, which is an empty array.

Here is the implementation in JavaScript:
```javascript
const modulo = 109 + 7;

function countMethods(queries) {
  const dp = Array(queries.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < queries.length; i++) {
    dp[i] = Array(queries[i][1]).fill(0);
  }

  for (let i = 1; i < queries.length; i++) {
    const ni = queries[i][0];
    const ki = queries[i][1];
    dp[i] = dp[i - 1].map((value, index) => {
      if (index === 0) return value;
      let count = 0;
      for (let j = index; j < ni; j++) {
        const product = value * queries[j][0];
        if (product <= ki) count++;
      }
      return (count + dp[i - 1][index]) % modulo;
    });
  }

  return dp[queries.length - 1].map(value => value % modulo);
}
```
Here is the explanation of how this code works:

* The `modulo` variable stores the modulus used for the calculations, which is the number of ways to construct an array of length `ni` with product `ki`.
* The `countMethods` function takes in a two-dimensional integer matrix `queries`, where each element `queries[i] = [ni, ki]` represents the length and product of the `ith` query.
* The function first initializes an empty dp table with the same size as the input array.
* Then it fills in the table by considering all possible values that can be inserted into the current position of the array. If the value is less than or equal to `ki`, we can add it to the product and recursively call the function for the remaining positions. If the value is greater than `ki`, we can skip it since it will not contribute to the final product.
* The base case is when `i = 1` and `j = 0`. In this case, there is only one way to construct an array of length 1 with a product of 0, which is an empty array.
* Finally, the function returns the dp table for the last row of the input matrix.

