A doubly linked list is a type of data structure that consists of a sequence of nodes, each of which has a reference (pointer) to the next node in the list as well as a reference to the previous node. In this program, we will create a doubly linked list where each node contains both a forward and backward pointer.

Here is an example of how you might implement this data structure:
```
// Define a node class that represents a single element in the list
class Node {
  constructor(value) {
    this.value = value;
    this.next = null;
    this.prev = null;
  }
}

// Define a doubly linked list class that manages the nodes
class DoublyLinkedList {
  constructor() {
    this.head = null;
    this.tail = null;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  // Method to add a new node to the end of the list
  push(value) {
    const node = new Node(value);
    if (this.tail === null) {
      this.head = node;
      this.tail = node;
    } else {
      this.tail.next = node;
      node.prev = this.tail;
      this.tail = node;
    }
    this.size++;
  }

  // Method to remove a node from the end of the list
  pop() {
    if (this.tail === null) return null;
    const node = this.tail;
    if (node.prev !== null) {
      node.prev.next = null;
    } else {
      this.head = null;
    }
    this.tail = node.prev;
    this.size--;
  }

  // Method to get the value of a node at a given index
  get(index) {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this.size) return null;
    let current = this.head;
    for (let i = 0; i < index; i++) {
      current = current.next;
    }
    return current.value;
  }
}
```
This implementation includes methods for adding and removing nodes from the list, as well as a method for retrieving the value of a node at a given index. The `Node` class represents a single element in the list, with properties for the value stored in the node, as well as references to the next and previous nodes in the list (if they exist). The `DoublyLinkedList` class manages the nodes and provides methods for manipulating the list.

To use this implementation, you can create a new instance of the `DoublyLinkedList` class and then add or remove nodes as needed:
```
const list = new DoublyLinkedList();
list.push(1);
list.push(2);
list.push(3);
console.log(list.get(0)); // Output: 1
console.log(list.get(1)); // Output: 2
console.log(list.get(2)); // Output: 3
list.pop();
console.log(list.get(2)); // Output: 2
```
This program will create a new doubly linked list, add three nodes to the end of the list, retrieve the values of the first and second nodes, remove the last node from the list, and then retrieve the value of the second node again. The output for this program would be `1`, `2`, and `2`.

