Here is a sample Java program that calculates the product of all the elements in the major diagonal, minor diagonal, central row, and central column of a square matrix:
```
import java.util.Scanner;

public class DiagonalProduct {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

    System.out.print("Enter the number of rows and columns for the matrix: ");
    int n = sc.nextInt();

    if (n % 2 == 0) {
      System.out.println("The input matrix must have an odd number of rows and columns.");
      return;
    }

    int[][] matrix = new int[n][n];

    System.out.print("Enter the elements for the matrix: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        matrix[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
      }
    }

    int majorDiagonalProduct = 1;
    int minorDiagonalProduct = 1;
    int centralRowProduct = 1;
    int centralColumnProduct = 1;
    int majorDiagonalPrimeCount = 0;
    int minorDiagonalPrimeCount = 0;
    int centralRowPrimeCount = 0;
    int centralColumnPrimeCount = 0;

    // Calculate the product of all elements in the major diagonal
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        if (i == j) {
          majorDiagonalProduct *= matrix[i][j];
          if (isPrime(matrix[i][j])) {
            majorDiagonalPrimeCount++;
          }
        }
      }
    }

    // Calculate the product of all elements in the minor diagonal
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        if (i + j == n - 1) {
          minorDiagonalProduct *= matrix[i][j];
          if (isPrime(matrix[i][j])) {
            minorDiagonalPrimeCount++;
          }
        }
      }
    }

    // Calculate the product of all elements in the central row and column
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      if (i == n / 2) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
          if (j == n / 2) {
            centralRowProduct *= matrix[i][j];
            if (isPrime(matrix[i][j])) {
              centralRowPrimeCount++;
            }
          }
        }
      } else if (j == n / 2) {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
          if (i == n / 2) {
            centralColumnProduct *= matrix[i][j];
            if (isPrime(matrix[i][j])) {
              centralColumnPrimeCount++;
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }

    System.out.println("Major Diagonal Product: " + majorDiagonalProduct);
    System.out.println("Major Diagonal Prime Count: " + majorDiagonalPrimeCount);
    System.out.println("Minor Diagonal Product: " + minorDiagonalProduct);
    System.out.println("Minor Diagonal Prime Count: " + minorDiagonalPrimeCount);
    System.out.println("Central Row Product: " + centralRowProduct);
    System.out.println("Central Row Prime Count: " + centralRowPrimeCount);
    System.out.println("Central Column Product: " + centralColumnProduct);
    System.out.println("Central Column Prime Count: " + centralColumnPrimeCount);
  }

  public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
    for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
      if (number % i == 0) {
        return false;
      }
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```
This program takes an integer `n` as input, which is the number of rows and columns in the square matrix. It then creates a two-dimensional array `matrix` with `n` rows and `n` columns, and prompts the user to enter the elements for the matrix.

The program then calculates the product of all elements in the major diagonal (i.e., the diagonal that runs from the top left corner to the bottom right corner), the minor diagonal (i.e., the diagonal that runs from the top right corner to the bottom left corner), and the central row and column (i.e., the row and column that contain the middle element). It also counts the number of prime numbers along these diagonals, rows, and columns.

Finally, it prints the product of all elements in the major diagonal, the number of prime numbers along the major diagonal, the product of all elements in the minor diagonal, the number of prime numbers along the minor diagonal, the product of all elements in the central row, the number of prime numbers along the central row, the product of all elements in the central column, and the number of prime numbers along the central column.

Note that this program assumes that the input matrix has an odd number of rows and columns, to ensure there is a central row and column. If the input matrix has an even number of rows or columns, the program will print an error message indicating that the input matrix must have an odd number of rows and columns.

