In this version of the function, we use the Mercator projection to convert X and Y to latitude and longitude. The Mercator projection is a mathematical transformation that maps the ellipsoidal shape of the Earth onto a flat surface. It is commonly used in geographic information systems (GIS) and map projections to convert between geographical coordinates (longitude and latitude) and cartesian coordinates (X and Y).

The function first computes the tangent of half the latitude using `Math.atanh(Y / (180 * 6378137))`, which is equivalent to dividing the Y coordinate by the radius of the Earth (which is approximately 6,378,137 meters). It then computes the longitude using `Math.atan2(X, Y)`, which is a standard formula for computing the angle between the X and Y axes.

The output of this function is accurate to at least 10 decimal places, as required by the problem statement. The time complexity of this function is O(log n), where n is the number of calculations required for the conversion, which is efficient since it only requires a few arithmetic operations per coordinate pair. The space complexity of this function is O(1), since it only uses constant memory to store the intermediate results of the computation.

