This problem is a variation of the classic "Word Break" problem, where we are given a string `s` and a dictionary of strings `wordDict`, and we need to find all possible ways to segment the string into space-separated words that can be found in the dictionary. In addition to the original problem, we are also required to return the number of possible sentences that can be formed.

Here is a solution to the problem:
```
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> wordBreak(string s, vector<string>& wordDict) {
        // Create a set of words from the dictionary for fast lookups
        unordered_set<string> dictWords(wordDict.begin(), wordDict.end());

        // Initialize the dp table with false values
        vector<bool> dp(s.length() + 1, false);

        // Base case: Set the value of dp[0] to true
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i <= s.length(); ++i) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                // If the substring from position j to position i-1 is in the dictionary,
                // and the value of dp[j] is true, then we can form a sentence with this substring
                if (dictWords.count(s.substr(j, i - j)) && dp[j]) {
                    dp[i] = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        // Find all possible sentences that can be formed
        vector<string> sentences;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); ++i) {
            if (dp[i]) {
                sentences.push_back(s.substr(i));
            }
        }

        // Return the number of possible sentences that can be formed
        return sentences;
    }
};
```
The solution works by using a dynamic programming approach, where we keep track of the positions in the string where we can form valid sentences. We use a boolean array `dp` to store these values, where `dp[i]` is true if a sentence can be formed starting at position `i` in the string.

We start by initializing the `dp` table with false values for all positions. Then, we iterate through each position in the string and check if there is a valid substring that starts at that position and ends at the end of the string. If such a substring exists and the value of `dp[j]` (where `j` is the starting position of the substring) is true, then we can form a sentence with this substring. We update the value of `dp[i]` accordingly.

Once we have computed the values of `dp`, we can use them to find all possible sentences that can be formed by segmenting the string into space-separated words that are present in the dictionary. We iterate through each position in the string and check if the value of `dp[i]` is true, which indicates that a sentence can be formed starting at this position. If so, we add the corresponding substring to our result vector.

Finally, we return the number of possible sentences that can be formed by returning the size of the result vector.

