Here is a possible algorithm to find the maximum subarray in an array of integers that contains at least one negative number:
```
public static int[] maxSubarray(int[] arr) {
    // Initialize variables
    int maxSum = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    int minNegativeIndex = -1;
    int startIndex = 0;
    int endIndex = 0;

    // Iterate through the array and find the maximum sum and the minimum negative index
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < 0) {
            minNegativeIndex = Math.min(minNegativeIndex, i);
        }
        maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, arr[i]);
    }

    // If there is no negative number in the array, return the entire array
    if (minNegativeIndex == -1) {
        startIndex = 0;
        endIndex = arr.length - 1;
    } else {
        // Find the first and last indexes of the subarray containing at least one negative number
        for (int i = minNegativeIndex; i < arr.length && arr[i] >= 0; i++) {}
        startIndex = Math.max(minNegativeIndex, i);
        for (int i = minNegativeIndex; i >= 0 && arr[i] >= 0; i--) {}
        endIndex = Math.min(minNegativeIndex, i + 1);
    }

    // Return the subarray
    return Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, startIndex, endIndex);
}
```
This algorithm first finds the minimum negative index in the array using a linear search. If there is no negative number in the array, it returns the entire array. Otherwise, it finds the first and last indexes of the subarray containing at least one negative number by iterating through the array from the minimum negative index to the end, and then from the beginning to the minimum negative index. It then returns a copy of the subarray using `Arrays.copyOfRange()`.

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the input array, because it only iterates through the array once to find the maximum sum and the minimum negative index.

