```java
public static void radixSort(int[] arr) {
    int n = arr.length;
    int maxDigits = (int)Math.log10(arr[n - 1]) + 1;
    for (int d = 1; d <= maxDigits; d++) {
        // Counting sort on current digit
        int[] count = new int[10];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int digit = (arr[i] / Math.pow(10, maxDigits - d)) % 10;
            count[digit]++;
        }
        for (int i = 1; i < 10; i++) {
            count[i] += count[i - 1];
        }
        int[] output = new int[n];
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int digit = (arr[i] / Math.pow(10, maxDigits - d)) % 10;
            output[count[digit] - 1] = arr[i];
            count[digit]--;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i] = output[i];
        }
    }
}
```
This function uses a combination of counting sort and radix sort to sort the input array. The `radixSort` function takes an integer array as input, and sorts it in ascending order based on the number of digits in the maximum number in the array. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(kn), where n is the size of the input array and k is the number of digits in the maximum number in the array. The space complexity of this algorithm is O(n + k), where n is the size of the input array and k is the number of digits in the maximum number in the array.

