Here is an example of how you could implement a priority queue using a max heap and a custom data structure:
```
class PriorityQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.heap = [];
  }

  insert(value) {
    const node = new Node(value);
    if (this.heap.length === 0) {
      // Insert the first value as the max value in the heap
      this.heap.push(node);
    } else {
      // Insert the new value in the correct position in the heap
      const parent = this.heap[this.heap.length - 1];
      if (parent.priority < node.priority) {
        this.heap.push(node);
        bubbleUp(this.heap, this.heap.length - 1);
      } else {
        // Insert the new value as a child of the current max value in the heap
        const index = parent.index;
        const leftChild = this.heap[2 * index + 1];
        const rightChild = this.heap[2 * index + 2];
        if (leftChild && leftChild.priority > node.priority) {
          this.heap.push(node);
          bubbleUp(this.heap, this.heap.length - 1);
        } else if (rightChild && rightChild.priority > node.priority) {
          this.heap.push(node);
          bubbleUp(this.heap, this.heap.length - 1);
        } else {
          // The new value has a lower priority than the current max value in the heap
          this.heap.push(node);
          const index = parent.index;
          bubbleDown(this.heap, index);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  deleteMax() {
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return undefined;
    // Remove the max value from the heap and update the parent's priority
    const max = this.heap[0];
    this.heap[0] = this.heap[this.heap.length - 1];
    this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return max;
    bubbleDown(this.heap, 0);
    return max;
  }

  getMax() {
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return undefined;
    return this.heap[0];
  }
}

class Node {
  constructor(value, priority = 1) {
    this.value = value;
    this.priority = priority;
  }
}

function bubbleUp(heap, index) {
  while (index > 0 && heap[index].priority < heap[Math.floor((index - 1) / 2)].priority) {
    const parent = heap[Math.floor((index - 1) / 2)];
    const child = heap[index];
    heap[index] = parent;
    parent.index = index;
    child.index = Math.floor((index - 1) / 2);
    index = Math.floor((index - 1) / 2);
  }
}

function bubbleDown(heap, index) {
  while (true) {
    const leftChild = heap[2 * index + 1];
    const rightChild = heap[2 * index + 2];
    if (!leftChild && !rightChild) break;
    let largerChildIndex = null;
    if (leftChild && (largerChildIndex === null || leftChild.priority > rightChild.priority)) {
      largerChildIndex = 2 * index + 1;
    } else if (rightChild && (largerChildIndex === null || rightChild.priority > leftChild.priority)) {
      largerChildIndex = 2 * index + 2;
    }
    if (largerChildIndex !== null) {
      const parent = heap[index];
      const child = heap[largerChildIndex];
      heap[index] = child;
      child.index = index;
      parent.index = largerChildIndex;
      index = largerChildIndex;
    } else {
      break;
    }
  }
}
```
This implementation uses a custom data structure called a "binary tree" to store the elements in the priority queue. The binary tree is implemented as an array, where each element in the array represents a node in the tree. Each node has a value and a priority, which determines its position in the heap.

The `insert` method takes an integer value as input and adds it to the priority queue. It does this by creating a new node with the given value and appending it to the end of the array. If the length of the array is greater than 0, it checks if the new node has a higher priority than the current max value in the heap. If it does, it bubbles up the new node to its correct position in the heap using the `bubbleUp` function.

The `deleteMax` method removes the highest priority element from the priority queue and returns it. It does this by removing the last element from the array and updating the parent's priority if necessary. If the length of the array is 0, it returns undefined. Otherwise, it bubbles down the new max value to its correct position in the heap using the `bubbleDown` function.

The `getMax` method returns the highest priority element in the priority queue without removing it. It does this by returning the first element in the array if the length of the array is greater than 0. Otherwise, it returns undefined.

The time complexity of the `insert`, `deleteMax`, and `getMax` methods are all O(log n) because they only require a single pass through the array to find the correct position for each operation. The time complexity of the `bubbleUp` and `bubbleDown` functions is O(1) because they only need to update the indices of the nodes in the heap, which takes constant time.

