You are tasked with implementing a generic data structure in Rust that allows for the creation of a singly linked list. The linked list should support the addition of elements at the front and back, as well as the removal of elements from the front. Additionally, the linked list should provide an iterator that allows for traversal of the elements in the list.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `LinkedList` struct and its associated methods. The `LinkedList` struct should be generic over the type of elements it contains. You should also implement the `IntoIterator` trait for the `LinkedList` to enable iteration over its elements.

The provided code snippet defines a trait `IntoRef` with an associated type `T` and serves as a starting point for the implementation.

Your implementation should include the following methods for the `LinkedList` struct:
1. `new` - creates a new empty linked list.
2. `push_front` - adds an element to the front of the linked list.
3. `push_back` - adds an element to the back of the linked list.
4. `pop_front` - removes and returns the element at the front of the linked list.
5. `iter` - returns an iterator over the elements of the linked list.

You should also implement the `IntoIterator` trait for the `LinkedList` struct to enable iteration over its elements.

Complete the implementation of the `LinkedList` struct and its associated methods to satisfy the requirements.

```rust
trait IntoRef<'a> {
    type T: Clone;

    fn into_ref(&'a self) -> Self::T;
}

struct Node<T> {
    data: T,
    next: Option<Box<Node<T>>>,
}

pub struct LinkedList<T> {
    head: Option<Box<Node<T>>>,
}

impl<T> LinkedList<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        LinkedList { head: None }
    }

    pub fn push_front(&mut self, data: T) {
        let new_node = Node {
            data,
            next: self.head.take(),
        };
        self.head = Some(Box::new(new_node));
    }

    pub fn push_back(&mut self, data: T) {
        let mut current = &mut self.head;
        while let Some(node) = current {
            current = &mut node.next;
        }
        *current = Some(Box::new(Node {
            data,
            next: None,
        }));
    }

    pub fn pop_front(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        self.head.take().map(|mut node| {
            self.head = node.next.take();
            node.data
        })
    }

    pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, T> {
        Iter { next: self.head.as_deref() }
    }
}

pub struct Iter<'a, T> {
    next: Option<&'a Node<T>>,
}

impl<'a, T> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
    type Item = &'a T;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        self.next.map(|node| {
            self.next = node.next.as_deref();
            &node.data
        })
    }
}

impl<'a, T: 'a> IntoIterator for &'a LinkedList<T> {
    type Item = &'a T;
    type IntoIter = Iter<'a, T>;

    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter {
        self.iter()
    }
}
```

