To find the kth smallest element in an array without sorting it and using only a constant amount of extra space, you can use a divide-and-conquer approach. The basic idea is to recursively partition the array into two halves until you reach the desired index k. Here's an outline of the algorithm:

1. If the size of the array is 1 or less, return the only element in the array (since it is already sorted).
2. Find the median of the array by selecting a random element from the array and partitioning the array around that element.
3. Recursively apply step 1 to the left half of the array and the right half of the array, until you reach the desired index k.
4. If the index k is in the left half of the array, return the kth smallest element from the left subarray.
5. If the index k is in the right half of the array, return the kth smallest element from the right subarray.
6. If the index k is not found in either the left or right subarrays, return -1 to indicate that the index is out of bounds.

Here's some sample Java code to illustrate the algorithm:
```java
public int findKthSmallest(int[] arr, int k) {
    // Base case: if the array has only one element or less, return it
    if (arr.length <= 1) {
        return arr[0];
    }

    // Partition the array around a random element
    int median = partition(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);

    // Recursively find the kth smallest element in the left and right subarrays
    int leftKthSmallest = findKthSmallest(Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, 0, median), k);
    int rightKthSmallest = findKthSmallest(Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, median + 1, arr.length), k - (median + 1));

    // If the index is in the left subarray, return the kth smallest element from there
    if (leftKthSmallest != -1) {
        return leftKthSmallest;
    }

    // If the index is in the right subarray, return the kth smallest element from there
    if (rightKthSmallest != -1) {
        return rightKthSmallest;
    }

    // If the index is not found in either subarray, return -1 to indicate that it's out of bounds
    return -1;
}

public int partition(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
    // Select a random element from the array as the pivot
    Random rand = new Random();
    int pivot = arr[rand.nextInt(end - start + 1) + start];

    // Partition the array around the pivot
    int i = start - 1;
    for (int j = start; j <= end; j++) {
        if (arr[j] < pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(arr, i, j);
        }
    }

    // Return the index of the pivot element in the partitioned array
    return i + 1;
}

public void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[j];
    arr[j] = temp;
}
```
This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the size of the input array. It uses only a constant amount of extra space, and it does not modify the original array or create any new arrays.

