Here is an example of a Java method that takes an integer as an argument, calculates its negative equivalent, and prints it:
```
import java.util.HashMap;

public class Factorial {
    private static final HashMap<Integer, Long> memoizedFactorials = new HashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = Integer.parseInt(args[0]);
        long negativeEquivalent = calculateNegativeEquivalent(number);
        System.out.println("The negative equivalent of " + number + " is: " + negativeEquivalent);
    }

    public static long calculateNegativeEquivalent(int number) {
        if (memoizedFactorials.containsKey(number)) {
            return memoizedFactorials.get(number);
        } else {
            long factorial = 1;
            for (int i = 2; i <= number; i++) {
                factorial *= i;
            }
            if (factorial > Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                return "The negative equivalent exceeds the maximum limit for integers";
            } else {
                memoizedFactorials.put(number, factorial);
                return -factorial;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This method uses a `HashMap` to store the previously computed factorial results and re-uses them when possible. The `memoizedFactorials` map is initialized with an empty HashMap, which is then populated as the method calculates the factorials for each number. Whenever the method encounters a new number that has not been calculated before, it calculates its factorial and stores it in the map.

The `calculateNegativeEquivalent` method takes an integer as an argument and returns its negative equivalent. If the factorial of the given number exceeds the maximum limit for integers, the method returns an error message instead. The method uses a loop to calculate the factorial of the given number, starting from 2 and multiplying it by each subsequent number up to the given number.

To make the code more efficient, the `memoizedFactorials` map is used to store and re-use previously calculated factorial results. This reduces the time complexity of the method from O(n) (where n is the input number) to O(1), since the method only needs to calculate the factorial once for each input number.

Note that the `HashMap` is initialized with a capacity of 0, which means it will grow dynamically as new elements are added. This allows the map to store an unlimited number of elements without requiring a fixed capacity.

