Here's an example of how you could use a more optimized prime checking function and add comments to the code:
```
// A function that checks if a number is prime
function isPrime(n) {
  // Check if n is less than or equal to 1
  if (n <= 1) return false;

  // Check if n is divisible by any numbers between 2 and the square root of n
  for (let i = 2; i < Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
    if (n % i === 0) return false;
  }

  // If we reach this point, then n is prime
  return true;
}

// A function that returns the sum of all prime numbers less than or equal to a given number
function sumOfPrimes(n) {
  let sum = 0;

  // Loop through all numbers less than or equal to n
  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    // Check if the current number is prime using the isPrime function
    if (isPrime(i)) {
      sum += i;
    }
  }

  return sum;
}
```
This code uses a more optimized prime checking function, which checks if a number is prime by dividing it by all numbers between 2 and the square root of n. This is an efficient way to check if a number is prime, as it eliminates the need to check for factors beyond the square root of n.

The code also includes comments that explain what each part of the function does, making it easier for someone reading the code to understand how it works and why it was written that way.

