To compare two arrays while ignoring any duplicate elements and taking into account the order of the remaining elements, you can use the following algorithm:

1. Iterate through both arrays simultaneously and compare each element to determine if they are equal without considering duplicates or case sensitivity. If the elements are not equal, return false immediately.
2. Keep track of a set of already compared elements to prevent duplicates from being considered in the comparison.
3. For each element in one array, check if it is a multiple of 5. If it is, add it to the set of already compared elements and move on to the next element.
4. If all elements are compared without returning false, return true.

Here's an example implementation in JavaScript:
```
function compareArrays(arr1, arr2) {
  if (arr1.length !== arr2.length) {
    return false;
  }

  const alreadyCompared = new Set();

  for (let i = 0; i < arr1.length; i++) {
    const element1 = arr1[i];
    const element2 = arr2[i];

    if (!element1.includes(element2) && !alreadyCompared.has(element1)) {
      return false;
    }

    if (element1 % 5 === 0) {
      alreadyCompared.add(element1);
    }
  }

  return true;
}
```
This function takes two arrays as input and returns a Boolean value indicating whether the arrays are equal without considering duplicates or case sensitivity. The function first checks if the two arrays have the same length, and if not, immediately returns false.

Next, it creates a set of already compared elements to prevent duplicates from being considered in the comparison.

The function then iterates through both arrays simultaneously using nested for loops. For each element in one array, it checks if the corresponding element in the other array is equal to it without considering duplicates or case sensitivity. If any element is not found, the function returns false immediately.

If all elements are compared without returning false, the function adds the current element to the set of already compared elements and moves on to the next element.

Finally, if all elements have been compared without returning false, the function returns true, indicating that the two arrays are equal without considering duplicates or case sensitivity.

