In this version of the code, we use a hash map (i.e., a dictionary) to keep track of the unique elements in the list. We initialize an empty hash map and then iterate through the list element by element. For each element, we check if it is already present in the hash map. If it is not present, we add it to the hash map with a value of `True`. This tells us that the element has not been seen before, so we add it to the list of unique elements.

The time complexity of this version of the code is O(n), where n is the length of the input list. This is because we only need to iterate through the list once and perform a lookup in the hash map for each element. The space complexity is also O(n) because we need to store the unique elements in the hash map, which requires O(n) memory.

Overall, this version of the code is more efficient than the original version because it reduces the time complexity and improves the space complexity. However, it may be less readable for some people due to the use of a hash map.

