Here is a sample implementation of a program that multiplies two given matrices:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>

// Function to multiply two matrices
std::vector<std::vector<int>> multiplyMatrices(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& matrix1, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& matrix2) {
  // Check if the dimensions of the matrices are compatible for multiplication
  if (matrix1[0].size() != matrix2.size()) {
    throw std::invalid_argument("Dimensions of the matrices are not compatible for multiplication");
  }

  // Initialize the result matrix with the correct size
  std::vector<std::vector<int>> result(matrix1.size(), std::vector<int>(matrix2[0].size()));

  // Multiply the matrices element-wise
  for (int i = 0; i < matrix1.size(); i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < matrix2[0].size(); j++) {
      result[i][j] = 0;
      for (int k = 0; k < matrix1[0].size(); k++) {
        result[i][j] += matrix1[i][k] * matrix2[k][j];
      }
    }
  }

  return result;
}

int main() {
  // Declare two matrices to be multiplied
  std::vector<std::vector<int>> matrix1 = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
  std::vector<std::vector<int>> matrix2 = {{5, 6}, {7, 8}};

  // Print the result of multiplying the matrices
  std::cout << "Result of multiplying the matrices: " << std::endl;
  for (const auto& row : multiplyMatrices(matrix1, matrix2)) {
    for (const auto& col : row) {
      std::cout << col << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
  }

  return 0;
}
```
This program defines a function `multiplyMatrices` that takes two matrices as input and returns their product. The function first checks if the dimensions of the matrices are compatible for multiplication, and then uses a nested loop to perform the element-wise multiplication. If the dimensions are not compatible, the function throws an `std::invalid_argument` exception with a suitable error message.

In `main`, two matrices are declared and initialized as 2D arrays of integers. The product of these matrices is computed using the `multiplyMatrices` function, and then printed to the console. If any errors occur during the multiplication process, such as attempting to multiply matrices with incompatible dimensions, the program will catch the exception and print an error message to the console.

