Here is an example of how you can check for potential overflow and underflow in C++:
```
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int x = 10, y = 20;
    if (x + y > INT_MAX || x + y < INT_MIN) {
        cout << "Possible overflow or underflow!" << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "No overflow or underflow!" << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code uses the `INT_MAX` and `INT_MIN` constants to check for potential overflow and underflow. If the sum of `x` and `y` is greater than `INT_MAX` or less than `INT_MIN`, then there is a possibility of overflow or underflow.

