This problem can be solved using dynamic programming. We will keep track of the maximum area of a submatrix that we have found so far, and update it as we explore different arrangements of the columns. The key insight is that if we rearrange the columns, the number of 1s in the submatrix does not change, but the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1 does change.

We will use a two-dimensional array `dp` to keep track of the maximum area of a submatrix that can be formed using the current arrangement of columns and the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1. The value of `dp[i][j]` will represent the maximum area of a submatrix that can be formed using the first `i` columns and the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1 in the first `j` rows.

We will initialize the array with the values from the input matrix, and then iterate through each row of the matrix and update the values in the array based on the current arrangement of columns and the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1. We will use a recursive function to compute the maximum area of a submatrix that can be formed using the current arrangement of columns and the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1.

The time complexity of this solution is O(m\*n), where m and n are the number of rows and columns in the input matrix, respectively. The space complexity is O(m\*n), as we need to store the values in the dynamic programming array.

Here is a sample implementation of the above approach:
```
class Solution {
    public int largestSubmatrix(int[][] matrix) {
        int m = matrix.length;
        int n = matrix[0].length;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];

        // Initialize the dynamic programming array with the values from the input matrix
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (matrix[i][j] == 1) {
                    dp[i][j] = 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
                }
            }
        }

        // Compute the maximum area of a submatrix that can be formed using the current arrangement of columns and the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1
        int maxArea = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (dp[i][j] > maxArea) {
                    maxArea = dp[i][j];
                }
            }
        }

        return maxArea;
    }
}
```
In the above implementation, we have used a dynamic programming array `dp` to keep track of the maximum area of a submatrix that can be formed using the current arrangement of columns and the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1. We have initialized the array with the values from the input matrix, and then iterated through each row of the matrix and updated the values in the array based on the current arrangement of columns and the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1. Finally, we have computed the maximum area of a submatrix that can be formed using the current arrangement of columns and the number of flips required to make all the 1s equal to 0 or 1.

This problem can be solved using a dynamic programming approach. The idea is to maintain a 2D array `dp` where `dp[i][j]` represents the minimum cost of moving the first `i` students so that they are in non-decreasing order, and the `j`-th student has height `heights[j]`.

The base case is when there is only one student, then the cost of moving it is 0.

For each subsequent student, we consider two options:

1. Move the current student to its correct position in the line. This will involve paying the weight of the current student.
2. Leave the current student in its current position and move the next student to its correct position. This will also involve paying the weight of the next student.

We can calculate the minimum cost for each option using the values in `dp` for the previous students. The final answer is the minimum cost of moving all `n` students to their correct positions.
```
int solution(vector<int>& heights, vector<int>& weights) {
    int n = heights.size();
    vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(n + 1));

    // Initialize the base case
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
        dp[i][0] = 0;
    }

    // Calculate the minimum cost for each option
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; ++j) {
            dp[i][j] = min(dp[i - 1][j - 1] + weights[j], dp[i - 1][j] + weights[j]);
        }
    }

    // Return the minimum cost for moving all students to their correct positions
    return dp[n][n];
}
```

